// ui::host — the contract both UI backends satisfy.
//
// Three additions are marked below (`loadDocumentText`, `setImageRewrite`, and `provideImage` taking a view rather than
// a raw pointer). 0.2.0's are marked with their version: `elementId`, `setElementAttribute`, `removeElementAttribute`,
// `elementChecked`, `findElement`, an event's `target`, one change per edit, `setInnerRml` releasing what it replaces,
// and `elementText` with no cap.
//
// TWO WAYS TO OPEN, ONE CONTRACT. `UiContext.open(width, height, fontPath)` is a STAGE: a fixed virtual resolution the
// program letterboxes and scales (a game's menus). `UiContext.openPage(width, height, fontPath)` (0.2.0) is a PAGE: the
// document fills the window and reflows with it, as a web page does (a tool, an editor). On the web a page's stage is
// the window itself — shown without `setViewport`, never scaled, resized by CSS alone, and an element with
// `overflow: auto` scrolls; natively the program passes its window's size to `setSize` on every resize and draws the
// commands at the window's origin, unscaled. Every method below means the same in both.
//
// ONE SET OF DOCUMENTS, TWO BACKENDS. `UiContext` is declared twice — in native.kama over @kama/rmlui and in web.kama
// over the browser's own DOM — under complementary `@compileFor` gates, so exactly one survives per build and a caller
// writes the bare type name with no erasure and no vtable. Nothing would notice the two drifting, which is what this
// contract is for: each declaration is checked against it where it is written, so a backend that renames a method or a
// parameter label fails at its own definition rather than at a caller that only compiles on the other target.
//
// `for value`: a `UiContext` is a copyable name for a UI whose state lives on the other side of the seam (RmlUi's C++,
// or the page's DOM), with an explicit `close`. That is what lets a program keep it in a module `static`, which may own
// nothing — a game built on this seam keeps half a dozen.
//
// THE UI RENDERS NOTHING. The native backend produces DRAW COMMANDS for the program's renderer (`render`, `command`,
// `texture`); the web backend's document is real DOM elements the browser paints, so it produces none. That is what lets
// one contract cover both — and it is why `render()` fills a list rather than putting pixels anywhere.
//
// EVERY STRING PARAMETER IS `const ref string`: the methods only read them, and `const ref` is what lets a literal be
// passed straight in. Take a literal wherever you can; bind a local only when the string is computed.

import { UiCommand, UiTexture, UiEvent, UiRect, std::collections::ConstView };
export { UiHost };

type contract UiHost for value {
    // Load another face after `open`. ⚠️ `fallback` IS NOT COSMETIC: a face missing a codepoint draws NOTHING for it
    // natively — not a box, not a blank — so load display faces first and a broad face last, as the fallback.
    // A face that will not load is reported (natively, in RmlUi's log), not fatal. A no-op on the web, whose faces come
    // from the page's own `@font-face` rules.
    fn void loadFont(const ref string path, bool fallback);

    // Load one document from a file and show it, focusing its `autofocus` element if it has one. False when it could not
    // be read or parsed. ⚠️ IT REPLACES the current document, and EVERY HANDLE `bindElement` returned dies with the old
    // one: re-bind after every switch. On the web the file is read from the program's virtual filesystem (an emcc
    // `--embed-file`) and rewritten to HTML by `ui::uidoc::rmlToHtml`.
    fn bool loadDocument(const ref string path);

    // ADDED: the same, from RML text the program holds (generated, embedded, or a test's). Same rules as `loadDocument`.
    fn bool loadDocumentText(const ref string rml);

    // Replace a bound element's CONTENT with markup — a variable number of rows, per-row styling — rather than text.
    // ⚠️ IT DESTROYS THE CHILDREN: bind containers, write into them, and RE-BIND WHAT YOU WROTE AFTER THE WRITE. Both
    // backends repair a re-bind (the same handle comes back, naming the new element), and a handle bound to the
    // container itself is untouched. ⚠️ IT IS A PARSER on both legs (RmlUi's, and `innerHTML`): text another person typed
    // goes through `ui::uidoc::escapeText` first — on the web an unescaped `<` is script injection, not a mangled screen.
    //
    // CHANGED (0.2.0): IT RELEASES THE BINDINGS TO WHAT IT REPLACES, on both legs. Every handle naming an element inside
    // lets go of it and of its listeners — so a list rewritten every frame leaks nothing — and is STALE: every call on
    // it is a no-op (false, 0, "", an empty rect), never a crash, until an element carries its id again. Its first use
    // after that (`bindElement`, or any call) names the new element with the same handle and listens there again. A
    // handle keeps its id (`elementId`) while the document lives.
    fn void setInnerRml(int32 handle, const ref string rml);

    // Hand over the pixels for one `<img>` a document will ask for. PIXELS, NOT A PATH: the backend decodes no image
    // format, and on the web nothing should read the file at all (the browser fetches its own copy). RGBA8,
    // PREMULTIPLIED, tightly packed, width*height*4 bytes, COPIED. ⚠️ `name` is what RmlUi resolves the `src` to: a
    // leading '/' is stripped, anything else is joined onto the document's directory — so `src="/assets/ui/badge.png"`
    // is provided as `assets/ui/badge.png`. ⚠️⚠️ PROVIDE BEFORE THE FIRST `loadDocument` THAT SHOWS IT: RmlUi resolves
    // an image during the load and records a miss permanently. A no-op on the web.
    // CHANGED: a `ConstView<uint8>` (was a raw `UnsafeConstPtr<uint8>`), so the length is checked.
    fn void provideImage(const ref string name, ConstView<uint8> rgba, int32 width, int32 height);

    // ADDED: when native and web read different FILES for one picture (natively a format the program decodes, on the
    // web one the browser does), the web leg rewrites every `<img src>` ending in `nativeExt` to end in `webExt` as it
    // inserts markup — documents and `setInnerRml` alike. ("" turns it off.) Both legs agree on the directory; only the
    // extension differs. A no-op natively. (For example `.pam` natively, `.webp` on the web.)
    fn void setImageRewrite(const ref string nativeExt, const ref string webExt);

    // Size in FRAMEBUFFER PIXELS, never logical points (on a Retina display or a browser with devicePixelRatio > 1 the
    // two differ). The web leg sizes its stage once, at `open`; this is a no-op there. A PAGE (`openPage`) calls it
    // natively on every window resize, with the window's size; on the web CSS follows the window and this stays a no-op.
    fn void setSize(int32 width, int32 height);

    // Where the document sits on screen, and whether it is on screen at all: a letterbox offset and scale in
    // framebuffer pixels. ⚠️ `visible` IS WHAT THE WEB HALF NEEDS: natively a screen goes away because nobody draws its
    // commands, but a backend that OWNS elements has no such signal. A no-op natively (the renderer applies the same
    // offsets to the recorded geometry). A PAGE is shown from the start and is never moved or scaled: there only
    // `visible` means anything (false hides it, true shows it again).
    fn void setViewport(float32 offsetX, float32 offsetY, float32 scale, bool visible);

    // Advance layout and animations, then rebuild the command list. Once per frame, in this order. `update` steps the
    // clock a fixed 1/60 s (deterministic, for headless runs); `updateAt` takes a monotonic clock in seconds, so hover
    // transitions and the caret's blink run in real time. Both no-ops on the web, which lays out and paints itself.
    fn void update();
    fn void updateAt(float64 seconds);
    fn void render();

    // The recorded commands and textures, valid between one `render()` and the next `update`. None on the web.
    fn int32 commandCount();
    fn UiCommand command(int32 index);
    fn int32 textureCount();
    fn UiTexture texture(int32 handle);

    // Which incarnation of a texture slot this is, from 1. ⚠️ A handle is an INDEX and a released slot is recycled, so
    // the same handle can come back holding a different atlas — and neither the size nor the data pointer can tell you
    // (two glyph atlases are often the same size; a freed buffer is often reallocated at the same address). A renderer
    // mapping these to GPU textures must key its upload on this.
    fn int32 textureGeneration(int32 handle);

    // The pointer, in framebuffer pixels. Buttons: 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle. The wheel in NOTCHES, positive down.
    // ⚠️ THE POINTER POSITION DECIDES WHAT SCROLLS: feed the cursor before the wheel. All three are no-ops on the web,
    // where the browser hit-tests, hovers and scrolls from the page's own pointer events (forwarding a second copy would
    // scroll twice).
    fn void mouseMove(float32 x, float32 y);
    fn void mouseButton(int32 button, bool down);
    fn void mouseWheel(float32 delta);

    // ---- the keyboard, which is a focus ring and a caret ----
    //
    // `code` is a UI_KEY_* (GLFW's numbering); the subset is in types.kama and anything else is dropped. The PROGRAM
    // gets every key first and forwards this subset — so a rebind screen or a game verb reading raw keys never competes
    // with the document. TAB walks the focusable elements, the arrows follow each element's `nav-*` (natively) or document
    // order (web), and ENTER CLICKS the focused element — so it arrives at `pollEvent` as an ordinary click and no
    // screen's handler changes.
    //
    // ⚠️ A NAVIGATION KEY WITH NOTHING FOCUSED LANDS ON THE FIRST FOCUSABLE ELEMENT rather than being lost, on both legs.
    //
    // ⚠️ ON THE WEB this assumes the page swallows the keys (preventDefault) before the DOM can act on them — which a game
    // does so SPACE and the arrows do not scroll the page — and moves the focus itself; releases are dropped there.
    fn void key(int32 code, bool down, bool shift);

    // Is the focus ring on a control the ring can reach? FALSE when nothing is focused, when only the document itself
    // is (RmlUi) or `<body>` is (a browser), and when no document is up. What lets ONE button mean both "press this" (the
    // ring holds something) and the screen's own verb (it does not).
    fn bool focusHeld();

    // Give the live document keyboard focus, so a pad can navigate without a mouse click first. Idempotent.
    fn void focusDocument();

    // ---- elements ----

    // Name an element ONCE and get an integer handle (>= 1); every event afterwards carries it. 0 when no element has
    // that id. Binding the same id again yields the SAME handle — and repairs it after a `setInnerRml` replaced the
    // element — while the document lives.
    fn int32 bindElement(const ref string elementId);

    // ADDED (0.2.0): name an element WITHOUT LISTENING to it. The same handle `bindElement` gives for the id (and an
    // event's `target` names), and every other method takes it, but nothing is ever queued for it — so a container
    // found this way does not hear its children either. For what a program only writes into or reads: a list it
    // rewrites with `setInnerRml`, a row it sets a class or an attribute on, a box it measures. 0 when no element has
    // that id. A handle already bound keeps listening, and `bindElement` on a found one starts it.
    fn int32 findElement(const ref string elementId);

    // ADDED (0.2.0): the id a handle names — one `bindElement` returned, or an event's `target`. "" for 0, an unknown
    // handle, or one from a document that has gone. What turns a row's handle back into the row.
    fn string elementId(int32 handle);

    // Where a bound element ended up after layout, in framebuffer pixels.
    fn UiRect elementRect(int32 handle);

    // A bound form control's value as a NUMBER (0.0 for an unknown handle, a non-control, or a value that does not
    // parse), and setting it. A float crosses as itself on both legs; no translation table is needed.
    fn float32 elementValue(int32 handle);
    fn void setElementValue(int32 handle, float32 value);

    // Switch one class on a bound element; a document rule on the class does the rest (hiding a row, greying a
    // control). In place — no subtree is destroyed — which is why it is safe where a rewrite is not.
    fn void setElementClass(int32 handle, const ref string name, bool on);

    // One inline style property ("width", "43%"; "" removes it) — a meter drawn without rewriting markup. True when
    // the backend took it.
    fn bool setElementProperty(int32 handle, const ref string name, const ref string value);

    // ADDED (0.2.0): set or remove one attribute — `src`, `disabled`, `hidden`, `checked`, a `data-*` a rule selects
    // on. In place, like a class. Two attributes are a control's STATE, and they write it on both legs: `checked` (a
    // browser's attribute is only the default once a person has toggled the box, so the web leg sets `.checked` too)
    // and `value` (written as `setElementText` writes it, quietly). On the web an `<img src>` gets `setImageRewrite`'s
    // extension, as markup does. ⚠️ NATIVELY, WRITING `checked` ON A CHECKBOX OR RADIO RAISES A CHANGE EVENT: RmlUi
    // dispatches one from the attribute itself and @kama/rmlui 0.1.0 has no quiet attribute write. A browser raises
    // none. ⚠️ `hidden` hides nothing by itself natively (RmlUi has no default stylesheet): the document says
    // `[hidden] { display: none; }`, which both legs then honour.
    fn void setElementAttribute(int32 handle, const ref string name, const ref string value);
    fn void removeElementAttribute(int32 handle, const ref string name);

    // ADDED (0.2.0): is a bound checkbox or radio checked? False for anything else, and for an unknown handle.
    fn bool elementChecked(int32 handle);

    // A bound text control's value, and setting it. ⚠️ NO CHANGE EVENT EITHER WAY, ON BOTH LEGS (nor for
    // `setElementValue`): a program writing state into a control is not a person editing it, and only a person's edit
    // reaches `pollEvent`. (RmlUi raises one for a range; the native leg drops it. A browser raises none.)
    // "" for anything that is not an `<input>`, `<textarea>` or `<select>`. CHANGED (0.2.0): no length cap on the web
    // (it was 511 bytes there); the whole value comes back on both legs.
    fn string elementText(int32 handle);
    fn void setElementText(int32 handle, const ref string text);

    // Pop one event, or None when the queue is drained. POLLED, never pushed: drain it once per frame, as input is.
    // Clicks and changes on bound elements. CHANGED (0.2.0): ONE CHANGE PER EDIT, the same count on both legs, for
    // every kind of control — each character typed into a text field or a textarea, each step of a range (live, as a
    // slider previews), each toggle of a checkbox, each choice of a radio or an option. A commit is no edit: ENTER in a
    // text field, a field losing focus, a slider let go, and the radio a choice unchecks raise none. ⚠️ ONE RESIDUE,
    // NATIVELY: RmlUi forces a change for every movement of a range's bar, so a step past either end, or a drag that
    // stays within one step, raises a change that changes nothing (@kama/rmlui 0.1.0 cannot tell it apart). A browser
    // raises none. ⚠️ EVENTS BUBBLE: a bound container also hears its children's clicks, so one
    // press can be two events (the button's, then the container's) — on both legs. Each names its `target` (0.2.0):
    // the nearest element with an id where it happened, so one binding on a container is enough to tell its rows
    // apart. ⚠️ POLL BEFORE YOU REWRITE: a target a `setInnerRml` destroyed in between reads as 0.
    fn Optional<UiEvent> pollEvent();

    // ---- focus and text entry ----

    // One typed character, as a codepoint from the platform's character event (after the keyboard layout). Natively it
    // goes to the focused text field; on the web the browser types into its own <input>, so this is a no-op there.
    fn void typeChar(int32 codepoint);

    // Put the ring on a bound element (true when it took it), and ask which bound element holds it (0 for none).
    fn bool focusElement(int32 handle);
    fn int32 focusedElement();

    // The ring is in a text control (<input type="text|password">, <textarea>): a printable key is typing, not a verb.
    fn bool textFocused();

    // RmlUi's `dp` unit: one dp is `ratio` framebuffer pixels, so text rasterises at its final size. A no-op on the web,
    // where `dp` became `px` in the stage and the viewport transform carries the scale.
    fn void setDpRatio(float32 ratio);

    // No document at all — every handle dies, as at `loadDocument`. Natively the close lands at the next update.
    fn void closeDocument();

    fn void close();
}
